3. USB HID Emulation & Why Report IDs Fail in PowerScribe
* Why Pure AHK Cannot Spoof USB HID: AutoHotkey injects events at user-mode via SendInput, whereas PowerScribe communicates directly with hid.dll to enumerate specific hardware Vendor/Product IDs (VID_0554 for PowerMic, VID_05F3 & PID_00FF for VEC Infinity Foot Pedal) and reads raw byte streams.
* The Multi-Collection Report ID Trap:
   * A genuine VEC Infinity foot pedal (VID 0x05F3, PID 0x00FF) has no Report ID (0x85) in its HID Report Descriptor.
   * On wire, it sends a pure 2-byte packet: [01 00] (Left), [02 00] (Middle), [04 00] (Right), [00 00] (Released).
   * Windows hid.dll prepends a leading 0x00 byte when no Report ID is present, handing PowerScribe a 3-byte buffer ([0x00, button_bitmask, 0x00]) where PowerScribe reads buffer[1].
   * If the foot pedal descriptor is merged into HID Remapper's main composite report descriptor using a Report ID (e.g., Report ID 4), Windows creates virtual child collections (Col01, Col04) and prefixes the packet with 0x04 ([0x04, 0x01, 0x00]). PowerScribe either attaches to the wrong collection (Col01 keyboard) or rejects the shifted report buffer.


________________


4. Single-RP2040 Consolidation Architecture (3-Interface USB Composite)
To run both HID Remapper and the VEC foot pedal on a single RP2040 without a second CircuitPython board or GPIO jumper loops:


1. Use Separate USB Interfaces (MI_00, MI_01, MI_02), NOT Report IDs (Col04): Windows usbccgp.sys splits separate USB interfaces at the bus level before hid.dll parses them.
2. Interface 0 (ITF_NUM_PEDAL = 0, EP 0x81): Dedicated to the 18-byte, Report-ID-less VEC foot pedal descriptor. PowerScribe attaches to the first matching interface (MI_00) and receives pure 2-byte packets (report_id = 0 in TinyUSB).
3. Interface 1 (ITF_NUM_HID = 1, EP 0x82): Standard HID Remapper composite Keyboard/Mouse output.
4. Interface 2 (ITF_NUM_CONFIG = 2, EP 0x83): HID Remapper Web Configurator (Usage Page 0xFF00).
5. Internal Mapping Trick: Keep an internal REPORT_ID_PEDAL (9) entry inside our_report_descriptor mapped to Button 17–Button 19 (0x00090011–0x00090013) so parse_our_descriptor() exposes the pedal buttons in remapper.org/config without colliding with standard mouse buttons (Button 1–Button 5), then intercept REPORT_ID_PEDAL in send_report() and transmit it out of Interface 0 with report_id = 0.


